        <div id="reading-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Read an ANCHEER Listing</h2>
<p>
            Every ANCHEER listing we looked at through the trackers contradicted itself somewhere. That is common
            among marketplace pads, and it is manageable if you know which figure to believe.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor</h3>
          <p>
            The TR6056 listing gives its motor as 1500 W in the copy, 1000 W in a comparison table and 2.5 HP in
            the technical details. The handlebar pad’s title says 3.0 HP and its feature list 1000 W. A 1000 W
            motor is about 1.3 HP if that is its continuous rating; the larger HP figures are peak or marketing
            numbers. For a walking pad, about 1 HP continuous is normal and sufficient. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the conversion and why peak figures mislead.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight capacity</h3>
          <p>
            The handlebar pad’s title says 320 lb and its features 350 lb. Plan on the lower figure, and leave a
            margin under it, since a pad’s frame and motor wear faster near their limit; our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline</h3>
          <p>
            ANCHEER’s "12%" and "15%" incline pads mostly use a manual incline: you stop, lift the front and set
            a stand in one of two positions. That is a fixed slope for the whole session, not a hill you can
            adjust mid-walk.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price</h3>
